{"packet_id":"b90c697f3bc56b9b","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether a consumer proposition is justified) from the suggested solution. It sets constraints (€20,000, 12 weeks, unknown buyer reach) and lists real alternatives, including no expansion and a change of route. It does not correct a premise or surface a missed constraint, such as the commercial-only scope of the distributor network. Alternative 5 also drifts back to B2B segments."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A9"],"reason":"T1 names who (homeowners), why (performance and convenience) and value capture (premium pricing). The chain rests on unsupported claims. 'Lower water consumption' appears in no source. 'Selling directly to consumers through its own retail channels' asserts a company capability the dossier never shows. Per the rubric, invented capability caps this dimension at 1."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (performance) and T2 (prestige) are rival motives for the same purchase, with a modest channel difference (design retailers in A10). Both assume consumer demand exists. Neither considers a no-demand or B2B-adjacent rival, so they do not imply clearly different products or customers."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"The predictions are observable, with explicit thresholds (15% intent at a 20% premium; a prestige-cited share). These are assumptions, not results. The subgroup 'households who already own a commercial dishwasher' is an odd and unjustified segment. Failure of the main prediction would trouble T1, but the link to whether investment is warranted is loose."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A15"],"reason":"Source classes are mislabelled: verified_observation sources are called 'independent_study' in A11, A14 and A15. A11 treats a commercial kitchen listing ('ideal for the busy kitchen') as support for consumer premium adoption. A12 labels configurability for commercial applications as supporting prestige while admitting it does not. A12 also describes the uses as 'consumer needs' when the source refers to commercial customers. The A15 limit text is copied from A14 and discusses prestige instead of T1. The relevant winterhalter-eu-formats source, found in A2, is never assessed."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A13","A14","A15"],"reason":"A13 searches against T1. A15 applies the premium-restraint observation as a contradiction to T1, and A14 applies it to T2. The trace does not explicitly report that the A13 search turned up nothing new against T1."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"The randomized messaging test separates T1 from T2 within budget. However, the decision rule proceeds with investment on a relative 5-point difference alone, with no absolute demand threshold. So it does not test the core question of whether to invest at all. Stated intent on simulated offers is weak evidence of demand. The inclusion criterion of 'ownership of a commercial dishwasher' is unjustified."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A18"],"reason":"The update correctly notes that SYN-D is simulated and concerns warehouse packing, not buyers, and justifiably retains T1. This is proportionate. It does not trace implications for the recommendation, though none were strictly needed."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"A19 recommends investing and piloting the UC-S at a 20% premium before any consumer evidence exists, while rating T1 'likely' with no supporting demand evidence. It is internally inconsistent: it calls the pilot 'direct-to-consumer' yet says it uses the existing distribution network, which the dossier describes as supplying the catering industry. It asserts unsupported features ('lower water consumption'). Uncertainty and reversal conditions are stated, but the position does not follow from the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A11"],"reason":"A commercial throughput listing for a busy kitchen is labelled support for consumer premium adoption."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"Configurability for commercial applications is labelled support for a prestige theory; the assessment itself concedes no relevance."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A9","A19"],"reason":"Product benefits ('lower water consumption') and company capabilities (own retail or direct-to-consumer channels) are asserted without any source. Hypothesized features are stated as facts in the value proposition."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A17","A19"],"reason":"The trace recommends investment and a priced pilot with T1 rated 'likely' despite no consumer demand evidence; the only relevant observation (A15) cuts against premiumization."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A11","A14","A15"],"reason":"Verified-observation sources are mislabelled as independent studies, and the A15 limit text is copied from the T2 assessment."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A16"],"reason":"The decision rule triggers investment on a relative messaging difference without any absolute demand threshold, so it cannot answer whether to invest."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete but contains material unsupported inferences: evidence mislabelled as support (A11, A12), invented product and channel claims (A9, A19), and an investment recommendation unsupported by any demand evidence (A17, A19). These are critical errors, so the trajectory is rejected despite a proportionate revision (A18)."}
